{"parentUuid":"bf9bad54-4712-4ba6-a0bc-11fff106dae6","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"0b8d936d-82c7-402f-ae06-0f556b62078f","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018TZG4KuNZ1aBbSatL89xrR","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t// ADR-001 Phase D — Benny runtime proxy.\n2\t//\n3\t// Forwards shell requests to the Benny FastAPI runtime (default\n4\t// http://127.0.0.1:8005) over two distinct, server-authoritative facades:\n5\t//\n6\t//   • /api/runtime/<path>        — HUMAN path. Strips /api/runtime, injects the\n7\t//     trusted BENNY_API_KEY, and *strips* any client X-Benny-Agent-Scope. A\n8\t//     human-driven request runs under Benny's normal RBAC (no agent scope).\n9\t//\n10\t//   • /api/agent-runtime/<path>  — AGENT path. Strips /api/agent-runtime,\n11\t//     injects the sandbox-bound BENNY_AGENT_API_KEY, and *sets* the agent\n12\t//     scope to \"sandbox\" server-side, overwriting whatever the client sent.\n13\t//\n14\t// Why two paths (ADR-001 confused-deputy fix): the determinism boundary used to\n15\t// be a client-set X-Benny-Agent-Scope header, so any authenticated caller could\n16\t// omit it and reach Benny with full trust. Scope is now bound to a credential\n17\t// the *proxy* injects, never to a header the browser controls. Benny's\n18\t// AgentScopeMiddleware additionally pins the agent key to sandbox scope so the\n19\t// boundary holds even if this proxy is misconfigured.\n20\t//\n21\t//   RESIDUAL GAP: both paths are same-origin, so in-page JS can still choose to\n22\t//   call /api/runtime instead of /api/agent-runtime. Closing that requires the\n23\t//   browser agent runtime to execute isolated from human JS (sandboxed\n24\t//   worker/iframe) — tracked in architecture/ADR-001 follow-up. This proxy\n25\t//   makes the boundary credential-bound; isolation makes it unbypassable.\n26\t//\n27\t// Configuration (env):\n28\t//   RUNTIME_BASE_URL     Default: \"http://127.0.0.1:8005\"\n29\t//   BENNY_API_KEY        Trust